# FigDown — figures as text. Spec: https://github.com/FigDown/figdown

figdown 0.4 sequence
title "All twelve interaction operators, on one session"

# ── What this figure is ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# `fragment … type=` takes one of TWELVE values, and the set is taken
# whole from UML 2.5.1's InteractionOperatorKind literals rather than
# picked from: `alt` `opt` `loop` `par` `strict` `seq` `critical` `neg`
# `assert` `ignore` `consider` `break`. Six of them are what Mermaid
# documents (loop, alt, opt, par, critical, break); the other six are
# what taking the enumeration whole buys, and a value nothing in the
# corpus writes is a value nobody has read.
#
# So this figure is the DEMONSTRATOR for the enum: one small session —
# a client opening a session with a service that keeps an audit log —
# with every one of the twelve stated on the part of that session where
# it is actually true. The subject is chosen to be ordinary on purpose;
# what is being shown is the OPERATOR, and an operator whose example
# needs a paragraph of protocol to justify it teaches the protocol
# instead.
#
# THE ORDER BELOW IS THE SESSION'S, NOT THE ENUM'S. A figure whose rows
# are alphabetical is a table, and this one has a time axis that means
# something. The enum order is in the paragraph above; the ladder order
# is: loop, alt, assert, neg, critical, par, strict, seq, opt, break,
# ignore, consider.
#
# EACH OPERATOR IS USED FOR WHAT IT MEANS, and the guard or the label
# says which meaning:
#   loop      the operand repeats; the guard carries the bound
#   alt       at most ONE operand runs, chosen by the guards
#   opt       one operand, run or not run
#   par       the operands interleave in any order
#   strict    the operands are TOTALLY ordered, across all lifelines
#   seq       weak sequencing — ordered per lifeline, not between
#   critical  the operand's occurrences may not be interleaved
#   neg       the operand's traces are INVALID — they must not happen
#   assert    the operand's traces are the ONLY valid continuation
#   ignore    the named messages may occur and are insignificant
#   consider  only the named messages are significant; others are not
#   break     the operand runs INSTEAD OF the rest of the enclosing run

# ── HONEST LIMIT — `ignore` and `consider` carry no argument ────────
# In UML both operators take a SET OF MESSAGE NAMES — `ignore {t, u}` —
# and that set is what makes the claim checkable. `fragment` has no
# argument slot, so the names live in the fragment's label, where they
# are prose: a reading agent can quote them and attribute them to the
# figure, and cannot parse them. The same is true of `loop`'s bounds,
# which UML writes `loop(1,3)` and this figure writes into the guard.
#
# ── HONEST LIMIT — one operator per frame, and no combination ───────
# UML lets an operand of one fragment hold another fragment, and this
# genre allows exactly ONE level of that. Twelve sibling frames is what
# a demonstrator of the ENUM should be; a figure that also nested them
# would be demonstrating the nesting. `dhcp-lease.fd` is where the
# nesting is shown, twice.

# ── Semantic classes ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Three roles, so a reading agent can group the messages by what they
# do rather than by which frame they sit in.
class session "Opens, uses or closes the session itself" stroke=#2563eb
class audit   "Written to the audit log — never part of the reply the client sees" stroke=#16a34a
class bad     "A trace this figure marks as forbidden or as a failure" stroke=#dc2626 style=dashed

lifeline c "Client"
lifeline s "Service"
lifeline a "Audit log"

# ── loop ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The operand repeats. UML puts the bound in the operator itself,
# `loop(1,3)`; here it is in the guard, which is the operand's own
# quoted string.
fragment open "opening the session" type=loop
operand tries "while no response, and fewer than 3 attempts made" in=open
message c -> s "POST /session  (credentials)" in=tries class=session

# ── alt ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# At most ONE operand runs, and the guards say which. Two operands is
# the minimum that makes the operator mean anything.
fragment auth "are the credentials accepted?" type=alt
operand accepted "accepted" in=auth
message c <- s "201 Created  (session token)" in=accepted class=session
state c "AUTHORIZED" in=accepted
operand rejected "rejected" in=auth
message c <- s "401 Unauthorized" in=rejected class=bad

# ── assert ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The operand's traces are the ONLY valid continuation at this point:
# every trace that reaches here and does something else is invalid.
# This is the operator that says MUST, and it is why `type=` has no
# default — a frame drawn with no operator would look like this one.
fragment mandatory "once a token is issued this MUST follow — nothing else is a valid continuation" type=assert
message s -> a "session opened  (subject, token id, time)" in=mandatory class=audit

# ── neg ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The operand's traces are INVALID: the figure draws the message in
# order to say it must never occur. Drawing a forbidden trace is the
# only way to name it, and the frame is what stops it being read as
# part of the protocol.
fragment forbidden "INVALID traces — the token must never travel in a query string" type=neg
message c -> s "GET /profile?token=…  (no TLS)" in=forbidden class=bad

# ── critical ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The operand's occurrences may not be interleaved with anything else
# on the lifelines it covers — a critical region, not a claim about
# duration.
fragment atomic "critical region — no other occurrence may interleave on these lifelines" type=critical
message s -> a "write: token issued" in=atomic class=audit

# ── par ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The operands interleave in any order, which is the ONE escape from
# this genre's total order: two messages that are merely adjacent are
# ORDERED, and only a `par` frame says they are not.
fragment concurrent "the profile fetch and the audit flush have no relative order" type=par
operand fetch "profile fetch" in=concurrent
message c -> s "GET /profile" in=fetch class=session
operand flush "audit flush" in=concurrent
message s -> a "flush buffered records" in=flush class=audit

# ── strict ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The operands are TOTALLY ordered across every lifeline they cover:
# nothing in the second may happen before anything in the first, even
# on a lifeline the two do not share.
fragment twophase "the two steps are totally ordered across BOTH lifelines" type=strict
operand prepare "1 — prepare" in=twophase
message c -> s "PUT /profile  (If-Match: etag)" in=prepare class=session
operand commit "2 — commit" in=twophase
message c <- s "204 No Content" in=commit class=session

# ── seq ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Weak sequencing: order is preserved on each lifeline separately and
# is unconstrained between lifelines. Here the two operands share the
# service, so the service orders them — the client and the audit log
# are unordered with respect to each other.
fragment weak "weak sequencing — ordered only on the lifeline the two operands share" type=seq
operand ask "on the client side" in=weak
message c -> s "GET /settings" in=ask class=session
operand log "on the audit side" in=weak
message s -> a "read: settings" in=log class=audit

# ── opt ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One operand, run or not run — `alt` with a single operand and no
# alternative to choose between.
fragment expanded "only when the client asked for the expanded form" type=opt
message c <- s "200 OK  (expanded profile)" in=expanded class=session

# ── break ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The operand runs INSTEAD OF the rest of the enclosing run: everything
# below this frame is abandoned when it fires. That is what makes it
# different from `opt`, which returns to the flow it interrupted.
fragment giveup "on 503 the remainder of the session is abandoned" type=break
message c <- s "503 Service Unavailable" in=giveup class=bad
state c "ABANDONED" in=giveup

# ── ignore ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The named messages MAY occur anywhere in this region and are not
# significant — so a trace that contains them and a trace that does not
# are the same trace as far as this figure is concerned. `<->` is ONE
# occurrence: an exchange in both directions whose individual messages
# are not enumerated, which is exactly what a heartbeat is.
fragment insignificant "ignore {heartbeat} — heartbeats may occur here and mean nothing to this figure" type=ignore
message c <-> s "heartbeat" in=insignificant

# ── consider ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The mirror of `ignore`: ONLY the named messages are significant here,
# and anything else that occurs is not. The two are not redundant —
# `ignore` names what to drop, `consider` names what to keep, and which
# one is shorter to write depends on the region.
fragment onlythese "consider {DELETE /session, 204} — nothing else in this region is significant" type=consider
message c -> s "DELETE /session" in=onlythese class=session
message c <- s "204 No Content" in=onlythese class=session
state c "CLOSED"
